ref 17592186352886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354090 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354090 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354090 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354090 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186351617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354090 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9cad37e7-767e-5799-b9ec-066a2117effb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354382 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354382 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354382 #edb/ref 1071 "The $12$ members of a student council are asked to elect among themselves a leadership team: a president, a vice-president, and a secretary. How many different leadership teams are possible?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99b2f516-70c2-5e55-a428-0efe2c5f0628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354382 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ members to choose from, so $n=12.$ We want to select $3$ of those members to be in the leadership team, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different $3$-member leadership teams is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =1\\,320.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354561 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(A)=0.5,$ $P(A\\cap B)=0.4,$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.8,$ find $P({B}^{′}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98c1f989-8fe6-518d-bf14-b7c905f21857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354561 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate $P(B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.8 & =0.5+P(B)-0.4 \\\\ P(B) & =0.8+0.4-0.5 \\\\ P(B) & =0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({B}^{′}) & =1-P(B) \\\\  & =1-0.7 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363451 #edb/ref 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90dab51f-a29c-5cbc-905e-4caff26f179f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363451 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $(t+1)^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n2t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186363453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363452 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "058071f4-384f-5a04-8070-2e272dac11f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363453 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d28fac4-221e-52aa-8a6e-3818e3db49fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363454 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi n" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363454 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363454 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19c48808-3086-5f2a-a3ee-a0a52eb69f63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363455 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363455 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363455 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93f0a18f-7b44-56a7-b9fd-13ab537d1472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363456 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363456 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363456 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ce2492d-8146-5e18-b179-26178cf72b0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363457 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi n}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363457 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363457 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28d5cfc4-fbbd-5602-abbd-34aea5298e13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386034 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386034 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(X∨Y)⇒Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca2063fd-762f-571b-86ef-906797fcc58c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386034 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(X∨Y)⇒Y≡¬(¬(X∨Y))∨Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(X∨Y))∨Y≡(X∨Y)∨Y.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the associative and idempotent law, we have\n\n$$\n(X∨Y)∨Y≡X∨Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∨Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186386037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386035 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "30b8ea65-fa59-5640-ad1e-d38a5f9bd046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386036 #edb/ref 1080 "X⇒Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "df3bb962-f2d1-5ee7-a113-b857fba5cef8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386037 #edb/ref 1080 "X∨Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "73685603-fc45-5a7b-ae0b-f79c6a725ab6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386038 #edb/ref 1080 "X∧Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386038 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386038 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "025fb47c-d3b8-5b43-bbc9-da0a019c987e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386039 #edb/ref 1080 "¬X∨Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386039 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386039 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20639ad4-5e0d-5759-9f30-7c348980aae9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386040 #edb/ref 1080 "Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386040 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386040 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d445084-5adf-5e8c-ba3f-39f72da70cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389811 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389811 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389811 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389811 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {2i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0ade82f-e346-52ab-87f9-cb9aa1c85b6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389811 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {1}={(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,1)$ to the point $(0.5,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c6aafd9ea78868e953340eaab3862491d275972d690e2ceb27c213dadbcaea6.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {2i}={(k,2i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,2i)$ to the point $(i,2i).$ \n\n![](images/02/02c8f93a25d7b510d3e238e6d4a974d6ccc2e83f3ac01fe92ce4535fbb4dce48.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/3b/3b3cefa03d71d99e4b7f3b6852ed36512d2a841a37c558034eac5c4b6870799c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
